# HTTP Methods

## 1. GET

**Answer:**
GET is used to retrieve data from the server. It does not modify resources.

### Example
GET /users/123

### Key Points
- Read-only
- Safe
- Idempotent
- Parameters sent in URL
- Can be cached

---

## 2. POST

**Answer:**
POST is used to send data to the server to create a new resource.

### Example

POST /users

Request Body:
{
  "name": "John",
  "email": "john@example.com"
}

### Key Points
- Creates new resource
- Data sent in request body
- Not safe
- Not idempotent

---

## 3. PUT

**Answer:**
PUT is used to update or replace an entire resource. If the resource does not exist, it may be created.

### Example

PUT /users/123

Request Body:
{
  "name": "John",
  "email": "john@example.com"
}

### Key Points
- Replaces entire resource
- Idempotent
- Data in request body
- Can create if not exists

---

## 4. PATCH

**Answer:**
PATCH is used to partially update an existing resource.

### Example

PATCH /users/123

Request Body:
{
  "email": "john.doe@example.com"
}

### Key Points
- Partial update
- Usually idempotent
- More efficient than PUT
- Data in request body

---

## 5. DELETE

**Answer:**
DELETE is used to remove a resource from the server.

### Example

DELETE /users/123

### Key Points
- Removes resource
- Idempotent
- Usually no request body
- Cannot be undone

---

# Quick Revision

### CRUD Operations

| Operation | HTTP Method |
|-----------|-------------|
| Create | POST |
| Read | GET |
| Update (Full) | PUT |
| Update (Partial) | PATCH |
| Delete | DELETE |

